# Lesson 6-4 — Solving Absolute Value Inequalities

Source: Module 6, Lesson 6-4, `Int1_0604_practice.docx`.

## Goals

- Solve absolute value inequalities. Rewrite them as compound inequalities with `and` or `or`.
- Graph solution sets on a number line.
- Model ranges and tolerances with absolute value inequalities.
- Identify no-solution and all-real-number cases.

## Examples 1, 2, 4, and 5 — Solve and Graph Absolute Value Inequalities

Problems 1 through 20 form one joint source section. The source does not divide these problems between the four example numbers.

For `|X| < a` or `|X| <= a`, with `a > 0`, write a bounded `and` inequality. For `|X| > a` or `|X| >= a`, with `a > 0`, write an exterior `or` inequality. A less-than inequality with a negative bound has no solution. A greater-than inequality with a negative bound has all real numbers as solutions.

The source expressions and verified solutions are:

1. `|x + 8| < 16` gives `-24 < x < 8`.
2. `|r + 1| <= 2` gives `-3 <= r <= 1`.
3. `|2c - 1| <= 7` gives `-3 <= c <= 4`.
4. `|3h - 3| < 12` gives `-3 < h < 5`.
5. `|m + 4| < -2` has no solution.
6. `|w + 5| < -8` has no solution.
7. `|r + 2| > 6` gives `r < -8` or `r > 4`.
8. `|k - 4| > 3` gives `k < 1` or `k > 7`.
9. `|2h - 3| >= 9` gives `h <= -3` or `h >= 6`.
10. `|4p + 2| >= 10` gives `p <= -3` or `p >= 2`.
11. `|5v + 3| > -9` has all real numbers as solutions.
12. `|-2c - 3| > -4` has all real numbers as solutions.
13. `|4n + 3| >= 18` gives `n <= -21/4` or `n >= 15/4`.
14. `|5t - 2| <= 6` gives `-4/5 <= t <= 8/5`.
15. `|(3h + 1)/2| < 8` gives `-17/3 < h < 5`.
16. `|(2p - 8)/4| >= 9` gives `p <= -14` or `p >= 22`.
17. `|(7c + 3)/2| <= -5` has no solution.
18. `|(2g + 3)/2| > -7` has all real numbers as solutions.
19. `|-6r - 4| < 8` gives `-2 < r < 2/3`.
20. `|-3p - 7| > 5` gives `p < -4` or `p > -2/3`.

For Problem 1, rewrite the expression as `-16 < x + 8 < 16`. Subtract 8 from all three parts to get `-24 < x < 8`.

For Problem 7, write `r + 2 < -6` or `r + 2 > 6`. Solve each inequality to get `r < -8` or `r > 4`.

For Problem 15, write `-8 < (3h + 1)/2 < 8`. Multiply all three parts by 2. Then subtract 1 and divide by 3 to get `-17/3 < h < 5`.

For Problem 19, write `-8 < -6r - 4 < 8`, and then write `-4 < -6r < 12`. Division by `-6` reverses both inequality symbols. The ordered result is `-2 < r < 2/3`.

## Example 3 — Model Absolute Value Inequalities in Context

Problem 21 states that the displayed speed of 60 mph is within 2.5 percent of the actual speed. Let `s` be the positive actual speed. The correct model is `|60 - s| <= 0.025s`. Its exact solution is `2400/41 <= s <= 800/13`. Thus, the approximate range is `58.537 <= s <= 61.538` mph.

Problem 22 gives `|t - 375| <= 25`, so `350 <= t <= 400` degrees Fahrenheit.

Problem 23 gives `|p - 130| <= 3.05`, so `126.95 <= p <= 133.05` fluid ounces.

Problem 24 treats 9 through 11 pounds as the inclusive healthy interval. The unhealthy values satisfy `|w - 10| > 1`, with `w > 0`.

Problem 25 gives `|s - 515| <= 114`, so `401 <= s <= 629`. Two stated standard deviations give `|s - 515| <= 228`, so `287 <= s <= 743`. These values reproduce the worksheet data. They do not state current SAT policy.

